# ArifiLabs: patches/ holds byte-exact artifacts, not ordinary text.
#
# `tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py series check` regenerates the series with `git format-patch`
# and BYTE-COMPARES it against what is committed. format-patch always writes LF. On Windows,
# where core.autocrlf=true is the Git-for-Windows install default, these files would be checked
# out as CRLF and the byte-compare could never pass on any clone - patch 0001 measured 61570
# bytes with 1132 CR against a freshly generated 60438 with 0, byte-equal after normalisation.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in both directions, so what git stores is what lands on
# disk, on every platform and in every clone. It is the correct setting for generated and banked
# patch files: the bytes ARE the artifact, and `git am` consumes them verbatim.
#
# `diff` is set alongside it so `-text` does not cost reviewability: git still renders these as
# text in diffs instead of reporting "Binary files differ".
patches/** -text diff

# ArifiLabs: git hooks are shell scripts executed by /bin/sh. Checked out with CRLF they fail
# on Linux and macOS with an unhelpful 'bad interpreter' error, and Git for Windows defaults
# core.autocrlf=true - so pin them to LF regardless of the cloner's settings.
.githooks/** text eol=lf

# HQ seat-58 2026-09-14: raw evidence receipts are byte-exact records; never eol-convert them (series replay parity)
r46b-*-evidence/** -text
